# AVIF encoding for Craft's image transforms.
#
# The base image's ImageMagick is built against libheif, so `queryFormats()`
# happily reports AVIF and Craft accepts `format: 'avif'` — but libheif ships its
# codecs as separate plugins, and without an AV1 *encoder* among them the write
# fails with "no encode delegate for this image format `AVIF'".
#
# That failure is the reason this file exists rather than a note in the README.
# Craft swallows it: the transform still lands on disk, still gets served as
# image/avif, and is JPEG bytes wearing an .avif extension. Browsers mostly sniff
# their way past it, so nothing looks broken — the AVIF <source> just quietly
# serves a file bigger than the WebP it was meant to beat. It is only visible to
# `file(1)`.
#
# aomenc over the alternatives (rav1e, svtenc): it's the reference encoder and
# the one that compresses hardest, and transforms are generated once and cached,
# so encode speed matters far less here than bytes on the wire.
#
# craft.frontend.supportsAvif checks for this at runtime rather than assuming it,
# so a host without the plugin serves WebP and nothing breaks.
ARG BASE_IMAGE
FROM $BASE_IMAGE

RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends libheif-plugin-aomenc \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
